13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171675 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171675 1077 "selection" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171675 1080 #uuid "306ddfad-0d89-5f5a-88e2-eb7ccee7713b" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171676 1081 "\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171676 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171676 1084 #uuid "9f6b7c49-a6aa-51c0-8666-631951ebcf09" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171677 1081 "e^{t/3}" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171677 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171677 1084 #uuid "c26fdb54-baf3-530d-b4e9-fb9ce8fa2c41" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171678 1081 "1 + e^{t/3}" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171678 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171678 1084 #uuid "cd63af06-af24-5515-9240-b6ef146fa71f" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171679 1081 "\\frac{2}{3}e^{2t/3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t/3}" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171679 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171679 1084 #uuid "f9c9aef7-34ec-5084-95f7-4800425fa149" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171680 1081 "1 + e^{t}" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171680 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171680 1084 #uuid "1ad5a717-2f6e-5312-bf5d-e920008f7dda" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171682 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171683 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171684 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171685 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1075 #edb/ref 17592186171686 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1077 "selection" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171681 1080 #uuid "12d1fd06-9549-53ed-8960-7b958b76dab0" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171682 1081 "4" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171682 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171682 1084 #uuid "8493c4a0-871c-5177-a611-79d399da4583" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171683 1081 "1" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171683 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171683 1084 #uuid "90ab2fa7-f0bb-5995-ad6a-bd36390a5a5c" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171684 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171684 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171684 1084 #uuid "d8825434-5542-58e1-8d22-d6f921f5fe04" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171685 1081 "3" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171685 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171685 1084 #uuid "1cf1d5fb-e457-5937-b9f7-3ecebe2aa922" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171686 1081 "0" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171686 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171686 1084 #uuid "658a6bb9-761f-5506-bb42-6b75e9c861d3" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171687 1069 "e-10051" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171687 1071 "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171687 1072 #uuid "59510c92-d9be-57c4-bc6d-497107401785" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171687 1074 "Since $n=100$ and\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}=0.64\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} = 100 \\cdot 0.64 = 64 > 5$, and\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 100 \\cdot (1-0.64) = 36 > 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.645) = 0.05\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n0.64 ± 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.64(1 - 0.64))/(100)}; 0.64 ± 0.079\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171688 1069 "e-10054" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171688 1071 "A survey of a random sample of $1\\,500$ U.S. households reveals that $780$ of them own at least one dog. Find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. households that own at least one dog.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171688 1072 #uuid "221d0cad-0cb8-5cdc-816e-19408a796d05" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171688 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one dog, and some don't,\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\, 500$, and\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one dog can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{p\\,} = \\dfrac{780}{1\\, 500} = 0.52\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} =1\\,500 \\cdot 0.52 = 780 > 5$, and\n- $n(1-\\widehat{\\,p}) = 1\\,500 \\cdot (1-0.52) = 720 > 5$.\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p} - p}{\\sqrt{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})/n}\\phantom{\\mid}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.025}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nwhere $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\n![](images/f5/f58f278f66244732c379f3ad0f064227298915b8e7c364b91b8766bb8789c1ea.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nz_{0.025}=1.96{:}\n$$\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.960}$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n0.52 ± 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.52(1 - 0.52))/(1500)}; 0.52 ± 0.025\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171689 1069 "e-10062" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171689 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n_1=29$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma_1=7$ and a sample of size $n_2=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma_2=9$. Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are $\\overline{x}_1=15$ and $\\overline{x}_2=9$, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171689 1072 #uuid "7d75f249-230d-5252-9db1-31f142ed7e70" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171689 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 15-9 = 6$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025$.\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$. As a result,\n$z_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{7^2}{29}+\\dfrac{9^2}{23}} \\approx 4.474$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$6 ± 4.474$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171690 1069 "e-10063" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171690 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n_1=68$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma_1=6$ and a sample of size $n_2=35$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma_2=5$. Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are $\\overline{x}_1=18$ and $\\overline{x}_2=15$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$, where $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.*" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171690 1072 #uuid "b8eff294-354a-5622-acfe-ca148de26225" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171690 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\geq30$, and\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances, $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n$\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 18-15 = 3$\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$z_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$E = 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{6^2}{68}+\\dfrac{5^2}{35}} \\approx 1.835$\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n$3 ± 1.835$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171691 1069 "e-10068" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171691 1071 "![](images/ea/ea72c9d4f965a6b3d16cedbc89760ee9b0a413b5836c481b081cf1b0e79c562d.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=32$ from a normal population. Given that the sample variance is $s^2=2.56$, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(X > 48.232) = 0.025$ and $P(X < 17.539) = 0.025$, where $X \\sim \\chi^2(31)$ has a chi-square distribution with $31$ degrees of freedom, as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171691 1072 #uuid "e51bdec4-dbdd-571f-acb8-950552957e84" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171691 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\n$$\n\nfollows a chi-square distribution $\\chi^2(n-1)$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population variance $\\sigma^2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{(n-1)s^2}{\\color{blue}b}, \\: \\dfrac{(n-1)s^2}{\\color{red}a} \\bigg)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(X > {\\color{blue}b}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X < {\\color{red}a}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $X \\sim \\chi^2(n-1)$.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nn = 32, \\qquad s^2 = 2.56\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(X > \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}48.232}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP(X < \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}17.539}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nb = {\\color{blue}48.232}, \\qquad a = {\\color{red}17.539}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population variance $\\sigma^2$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(((32 - 1) \\cdot 2.56)/(48.232), ((32 - 1) \\cdot 2.56)/(17.539)) = (1.645, 4.525)\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171692 1069 "e-10069" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171692 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=37$ from a normal population. Given that the sample standard deviation is $s=1.5$, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(X > 54.437) = 0.025$ and $P(X < 21.336) = 0.025$, where $X \\sim \\chi^2(36)$ has a chi-square distribution with $36$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171692 1072 #uuid "7c30468c-0b93-57d8-987c-88702023830b" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171692 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\n$$\n\nfollows a chi-square distribution $\\chi^2(n-1)$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population variance $\\sigma^2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{(n-1)s^2}{\\color{blue}b}, \\: \\dfrac{(n-1)s^2}{\\color{red}a} \\bigg)\n$$\n\nand the corresponding confidence interval for the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{s\\sqrt{n-1}}{\\sqrt{\\color{blue}b}}, \\: \\dfrac{s\\sqrt{n-1}}{\\sqrt{\\color{red}a}} \\bigg)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(X > {\\color{blue}b}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X < {\\color{red}a}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $X \\sim \\chi^2(n-1)$.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nn = 37, \\qquad s = 1.5\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(X > \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}54.437}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP(X < \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}21.336}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nb = {\\color{blue}54.437}, \\qquad a = {\\color{red}39.336}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d326008134123ccd0d5cfe5f083a3eead9ffc142251812d0f40eb7cfbbec437.png)\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{37 - 1}}{\\sqrt{54.437}}, \\frac{1.5 \\cdot \\sqrt{37 - 1}}{\\sqrt{21.336}}) &= (1.220, 1.948)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171693 1069 "e-10070" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171693 1071 "A sample of $30$ high school students was surveyed on the number of hours they spent playing video games last week. The sample standard deviation was $2.56\\, \\textrm{h}$. Assuming the number of hours playing video games is normally distributed among the population under consideration, find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population standard deviation corresponding to all students in this school.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $X$ that has a chi-square distribution $\\chi^2(\\nu)$ with $\\nu$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the values $\\chi_{\\nu,p}$ such that $P(X > \\chi_{\\nu,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $\\nu$ and $p$.*\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $29$ | $\\,13.121\\,$ | $\\,16.047\\,$ | $\\,17.708\\,$ | $\\,42.557\\,$ | $\\,45.722\\,$ | $\\,52.336\\,$ |\n| $30$ | $\\,13.787\\,$ | $\\,16.791\\,$ | $\\,18.493\\,$ | $\\,43.773\\,$ | $\\,46.979\\,$ | $\\,53.672\\,$ |\n| $31$ | $\\,14.458\\,$ | $\\,17.539\\,$ | $\\,19.281\\,$ | $\\,44.985\\,$ | $\\,48.232\\,$ | $\\,55.003\\,$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171693 1072 #uuid "f6482ab1-1822-542d-91a2-7f612a3797ea" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171693 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\n$$\n\nfollows a chi-square distribution $\\chi^2(n-1)$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $S^2$ is the sample variance, and\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population variance $\\sigma^2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{(n-1)s^2}{\\color{blue}b}, \\: \\dfrac{(n-1)s^2}{\\color{red}a} \\bigg)\n$$\n\nand the corresponding confidence interval for the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{s\\sqrt{n-1}}{\\sqrt{\\color{blue}b}}, \\: \\dfrac{s\\sqrt{n-1}}{\\sqrt{\\color{red}a}} \\bigg)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(X > {\\color{blue}b}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nP(X < {\\color{red}a}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $X \\sim \\chi^2(n-1)$.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nn = 30, \\qquad s = 2.56\\, \\textrm{h}\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the values $a$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X > {\\color{blue}b}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP(X < {\\color{red}a}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ has a chi-square distribution with\n\n$$\n\\nu = n-1=30-1=29\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/7f/7f6857e860a9399fc6c5d97585ce0b101707fc6a25b513bdaf82a2dc4ac82599.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the chi-square distribution, we obtain that\n\n$$\nb = {\\color{blue}45.722}{:}\n$$\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.050$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.025}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} 29}$ | $\\,13.121\\,$ | $\\,16.047\\,$ | $\\,17.708\\,$ | $\\,42.557\\,$ | $\\,\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}45.722}\\,$ | $\\,52.336\\,$ |\n| $30$ | $\\,13.787\\,$ | $\\,16.791\\,$ | $\\,18.493\\,$ | $\\,43.773\\,$ | $\\,46.979\\,$ | $\\,53.672\\,$ |\n| $31$ | $\\,14.458\\,$ | $\\,17.539\\,$ | $\\,19.281\\,$ | $\\,44.985\\,$ | $\\,48.232\\,$ | $\\,55.003\\,$ |\n\nAlso, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > a) &= 1 - P(X < a) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.025 \\\\\n&= 0.975\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfrom the same table, we get that\n\n$$\na = {\\color{red}16.047}{:}\n$$\n\n| $\\nu \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}0.975}$ | $0.950$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}29}$ | $\\,13.121\\,$ | $\\,\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}16.047}\\,$ | $\\,17.708\\,$ | $\\,42.557\\,$ | $\\,45.722\\,$ | $\\,52.336\\,$ |\n| $30$ | $\\,13.787\\,$ | $\\,16.791\\,$ | $\\,18.493\\,$ | $\\,43.773\\,$ | $\\,46.979\\,$ | $\\,53.672\\,$ |\n| $31$ | $\\,14.458\\,$ | $\\,17.539\\,$ | $\\,19.281\\,$ | $\\,44.985\\,$ | $\\,48.232\\,$ | $\\,55.003\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population standard deviation $\\sigma$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2.56 \\cdot \\sqrt{30 - 1}}{\\sqrt{45.722}}, \\frac{2.56 \\cdot \\sqrt{30 - 1}}{\\sqrt{16.047}}) = (2.039, \\mid 3.441)\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171694 1069 "e-10094" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171694 1071 "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.25$, and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is given by $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$.*" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171694 1072 #uuid "416faff8-3422-5bb0-9062-571ccfb18093" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171694 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7 x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y}=1.6+0.7 x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 0.7$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{6,0.05} = 1.943$.\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 1.943 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.25}{42}} \\approx 0.3$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 0.7 \\pm 0.3$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171695 1069 "e-10999" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171695 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\sin{x}$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The zeros of the function are $x=2n\\pi$.\n2. The function has minimum values at $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+2n\\pi$.\n3. The function has maximum values at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+2n\\pi$." 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171695 1072 #uuid "6646abbd-4248-539a-9656-0c0e2cf21d74" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171695 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\sin{x}$.\n\n![](images/1d/1dfe4444da1a8f30db08c90de8efecbe1214125b1cfbf49c2ae8681edf3d10db.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The zeros occur at $x=0, \\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots$, which can be written as $x=n\\pi$.\n- Statement II is true. The function has a minimum at $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, and the period is $2\\pi$. Therefore, the minimum values occur at $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+2n\\pi$.\n- Statement III is true. The function has a maximum at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, and the period is $2\\pi$. Therefore, the maximum values occur at $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+2n\\pi$." 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171696 1069 "e-11000" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171696 1071 "If $\\sin x = \\dfrac{1}2$, then what is $-2\\sin(x+6\\pi)-3$?" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171696 1072 #uuid "b48b1e81-1bfa-5236-b43d-edf2c44503d2" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171696 1074 "The period of $y = \\sin x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\sin{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= \\sin (x + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\sin (x + 6π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sin{(x+6\\pi)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\sin (x + 6π) - 3 &= -2(\\frac{1}{2}) - 3 \\\\\n&=-1 - 3 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171697 1069 "e-11001" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171697 1071 "If $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac{4}{7}$, then what is the value of $\\sin(-x + 16 \\pi)$?" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171697 1072 #uuid "5aeb80cf-cd16-5823-977f-60dbadbdab73" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171697 1074 "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sin{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sin{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\sin{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\sin(-x) = -\\sin{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x + 16π)|= \\sin (-x + 2π \\cdot 8); = \\sin (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x)|=-\\sin x; = - (-\\frac{4}{7}); = \\frac{4}{7}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x + 16\\pi) = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171698 1069 "e-11094" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171698 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=45$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7$. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test at a $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu$ is smaller than $18$.\n\nGiven that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=17$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The **one-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\leq -2.576$\n2. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\lt 18$\n3. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\lt 18$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171698 1072 #uuid "894d1292-ce58-5e80-8801-47dc432380e6" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171698 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample means is also normal, namely,\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu = 18$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu \\lt 18$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e., $\\mu=18$, we compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - μ)/(σ/\\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{17 - 18}{7/\\sqrt{45}} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.958\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $5\\%$ one-tailed critical value is $z \\approx 1.645$. $p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$z_p$$1.282$$\\color{blue}1.645$$1.960$$2.326$$2.576$However, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu \\lt 18$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the normal distribution. So, our critical region is $Z \\leq -1.645$.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Our test statistic ($-0.958$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/d1/d15fac99271cfad60919827f94fa2d4497bfce4ad79a3783dab7f21931536791.png)\nSo, we do not reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **insufficient** evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu \\lt 18$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171699 1069 "e-11095" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171699 1071 "Consider two independent samples of sizes $n_x=35$ and $n_y=15$ from normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with standard deviations $\\sigma_x=2$ and $\\sigma_y=3$, respectively. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nGiven that the sample means are $\\overline{x}=6$ and $\\overline{y}=7$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The $5\\%$ **one-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\le -1.960$\n2. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$\n3. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171699 1072 #uuid "0c553cab-a800-501b-808f-29c4b006b47a" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171699 1074 "We are told that the populations are normally distributed and the samples are independent. Therefore, the distribution of the difference of sample means is also normal, namely,\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x-\\mu_y, \\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}\\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances, and\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mu_x-\\mu_y=0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{σ_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{σ_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (6 - 7 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{2^{2}}{35} + \\frac{3^{2}}{15}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.183\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $5\\%$ one-tailed critical value is $z \\approx 1.645$.\n$p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$z_p$$1.282$$\\color{blue} 1.645$$1.960$$2.326$$2.576$\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the normal distribution. So, our critical region is $Z \\leq -1.645$.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Our test statistic ($-1.183$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/a0/a01041c7be6c692ed434b3af9574eb49969ee1cadd0c957c284a8305876708c3.png)\nSo, we do not reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **insufficient** evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171700 1069 "e-11096" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171700 1071 "Consider two independent samples of sizes $n_x = 82$ and $n_y = 71$ from populations $X$ and $Y$ with standard deviations $\\sigma_x = 12$ and $\\sigma_y = 9$, respectively. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $5 \\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nGiven that the sample means are $\\overline{x}=12$ and $\\overline{y}=17$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The $5\\%$ critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\le -1.645$\n2. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$\n3. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171700 1072 #uuid "0254b70f-3c6b-556e-8b19-bab72400fe14" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171700 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the samples are independent, and the sample sizes\n\n$$\nn_x = 82 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn_y = 71 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nare *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x-\\mu_y, \\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}\\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are the population variances, and\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mu_x-\\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{σ_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{σ_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (12 - 17 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{12^{2}}{82} + \\frac{9^{2}}{71}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 2.938\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the table, the $5 \\%$ one-tailed critical value is $z \\approx 1.645$.\n$p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$z_p$$1.282$$\\color{blue}1.645$$1.960$$2.326$$2.576$\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the normal distribution. So, our critical region is $Z \\leq -1.645$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Our test statistic ($-2.938$) lies in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/db/dbac6e7bbfb87002719110149d8d4ce4ba7e9a3c0958a510f5204234592eb484.png)\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **sufficient** evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\lt \\mu_y$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171701 1069 "e-11097" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171701 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n = 250$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma = 7$. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $2.5 \\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu$ is smaller than $10$.\n\nGiven that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x} = 9$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\leq -2.576$\n2. At the $2.5 \\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\lt 10$\n3. At the $2.5 \\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\lt 10$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171701 1072 #uuid "9d0d2e1e-a71b-57e3-aa89-bac61ff29dce" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171701 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 250 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, the distribution of sample means is also approximately normal. In other words, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu = 10$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu \\lt 10$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e., $\\mu = 10$, we compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - μ)/(σ/\\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{9 - 10}{7/\\sqrt{250}} \\\\\n&\\approx - 2.259\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $2.5 \\%$ one-tailed critical value is $z \\approx 1.960$.\n$p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$z_p$$1.282$$1.645$$\\color{blue}1.960$$2.326$$2.576$\nSince the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu \\lt 10$, we must consider the left tail. So, our critical region is\n$Z \\leq -1.960$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Our test statistic ($-2.259$) lies in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/dc/dc71f9401109e4acc0766532db2dd46f19258c7791124ff43c18dacf293fdee3.png)\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **sufficient** evidence that, at the $2.5 \\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu \\lt 10$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171702 1069 "e-11098" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171702 1071 "| **Hours ($x$)** | **$4$** | **$4.5$** | **$5.5$** | **$6$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **Grades ($y$)** | $62$ | $65$ | $77$ | $81$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they finally obtained on that exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5$, and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375$, find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by*\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$." 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171702 1072 #uuid "4d33f1a3-c51f-5a97-a498-def7a8364d81" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171702 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n- $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x = 21.3 + 10x\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the slope $\\beta_1$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: \\overbrace{t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]}^{\\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n- $P(T > t_{n-2,\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n- $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,]$ is the standard error of $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$, given by\n$\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}$\nwhere we have:$\\textrm{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n$\\textrm{MSE} = \\dfrac{1}{n-2}\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$\nwhere $\\widehat{\\,y}_i$ is the estimate of $y_i$ given by our regression model.$S_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n$\\displaystyle S_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x$, our point estimate is\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 = 10$.\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have\n$\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05$.\nWe are given that $P(T > 2.92) = 0.05$. As a result,\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} = t_{2,0.05} = 2.92$.Computing $S_{xx}$, we get\n$∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))^{2}|= (4 - 5)^{2} + (4.5 - 5)^{2} + (5.5 - 5)^{2} + (6 - 5)^{2}; = 1 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 1; = 2.5$. Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n$t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 2.92 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1.375}{2.5}} \\approx 2.2$\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n$\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1 \\: \\pm \\: t_{n-2,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = 10 \\pm 2.2$" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171703 1069 "e-11099" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171703 1071 "The manufacturer claims that the weight of a particular chocolate bar has population mean $\\mu = 50 \\, \\textrm{g}$ and population standard deviation of $\\sigma = 5 \\, \\textrm{g}$. An inspector selected $150$ bars of chocolate and found that their mean weight is $49.3 \\, \\textrm{g}$.\nThey wish to carry out a hypothesis test to determine whether there is sufficient evidence to challenge the manufacturer's claim.\n\nBy carrying out a hypothesis test at a $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu$ does not equal $50 \\, \\textrm{g}$, determine which of the following statements are true:\n\n1. The critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\leq -1.645$ or $Z \\geq 1.645$.\n2. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\neq 50 \\, \\textrm{g}$\n3. At the $5\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\neq 50 \\, \\textrm{g}$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171703 1072 #uuid "a4e25e28-8277-5488-af0f-09a5a2edfff6" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171703 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size\n\n$$\nn = 150 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nis *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, the distribution of sample means is also approximately normal. In other words, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu = 50$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu \\neq 50$ is the alternative (two-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = 49.3 \\, \\textrm{g} \\qquad n = 150, \\qquad \\sigma = 5 \\, \\textrm{g}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e., $\\mu=50$, we compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - μ)/(σ/\\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{49.3 - 50}{5/\\sqrt{150}} \\\\\n&=-1.715\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the table, the $5\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the $5\\% \\div 2 = 2.5\\%$ *one-tailed* critical value) is $z \\approx 1.960$.\n$p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$z_p$$1.282$$1.645$$\\color{blue}1.960$$2.326$$2.576$\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n$Z \\leq -1.960 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad Z \\geq 1.960$.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Our test statistic ($-1.715$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/99/999b3d2ea661e2c5fc0679d3927e91c211311e50fc8b6b39766de8a0bb1e288c.png)\nSo, we can't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **insufficient** evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu \\neq 50$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171704 1069 "e-11100" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171704 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=50$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=12$. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ level of significance to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu$ does not equal $26$.\n\nGiven that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=25$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\leq -1.645$ or $Z \\geq 1.645$\n2. At the $10\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\neq 26$\n3. At the $10\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu \\neq 26$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171704 1072 #uuid "f0e200d8-46cd-5524-afaf-3022faecebe8" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171704 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample means is also normal, namely,\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nand we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\sim N(0,1)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu = 26$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu \\neq 26$ is the alternative (two-tailed) hypothesis\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e., $\\mu=26$, we compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz &= (x^(―) - μ)/(σ/\\sqrt{n}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{25 - 26}{12/\\sqrt{50}} \\\\\n&\\approx - 0.589\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the table, the $5\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the $10\\% \\div 2 = 5\\%$ *one-tailed* critical value) is $z \\approx 1.645$.\n$p$$0.100$$0.050$$0.025$$0.010$$0.005$$z_p$$1.282$$\\color{blue}1.645$$1.960$$2.326$$2.576$\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n$Z \\leq -1.645 \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad Z \\geq 1.645$.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Our test statistic ($-0.589$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/0c/0c68f367cbc7816b62b634b39bb46eebab8a2de852f414e1b250b91d811e36c1.png)\nSo, we do not reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **insufficient** evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu \\neq 26$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171705 1069 "e-11101" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171705 1071 "Consider two independent samples of sizes $n_x=12$ and $n_y=15$ from normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with standard deviations $\\sigma_x=2$ and $\\sigma_y=4$, respectively. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is not equal to the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nGiven that the sample means are $\\overline{x}=25$ and $\\overline{y}=24$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The $10\\%$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\le -1.645$ or $Z\\ge 1.645$\n2. At the $10\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$\n3. At the $10\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particula