statement III is false. Our test statistic ($0.845$) does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.![](images/64/6490d8968ffe0ba1d6a99dd06d9fc3787868c7abfa413fa4883e07793d20d738.png)\nSo, we don't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:$\\qquad$ *There is **insufficient** evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$.*\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171706 1069 "e-11102" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171706 1071 "Previous studies show that the scores of men and women on a particular test have standard deviations of $17$ points and $14$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean $\\mu_x$ for men is the same as the population mean $\\mu_y$ for women. They decide to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level.\n\nGiven that the scientists sampled $150$ men and $125$ women and found that their mean scores on the test are $99.7$ points and $101.2$ points, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The $10\\%$ critical region for the test statistic is approximately $Z \\le -1.645$ or $Z \\ge 1.645$\n2. At the $10\\%$ level of significance, there is **insufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$\n3. At the $10\\%$ level of significance, there is **sufficient** evidence that $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171706 1072 #uuid "a4c00fc7-746a-5d81-8da3-d2f0e2867622" 13194139533368 17592186045585 true] [17592186171706 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sa